# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cp1252-codec-host-independent — provide CP-1252/Windows-1252 without relying on the host CL codec
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: portability
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: schms-ci-and-p3

* Context

OPEN with encoding =CP-1252= / =WINDOWS-1252= (and the CLI/-E paths that
resolve to =:windows-1252=) delegates to the host CL's external-format:
=(open path :external-format :windows-1252)=. SBCL ships that codec; CCL
does NOT — =ccl= has no windows-1252 / cp1252 character-encoding at all
(=lookup-character-encoding= returns nil; opening a stream with it signals).

* Problem

On CCL, =(open f "w" "CP-1252")= / =(open f "w,ccs=CP-1252")= errors
(AUTOLISP-RUNTIME-ERROR) instead of writing CP-1252 bytes. This blocked a
green CCL lane (ccl-suite-failures.issue). Stopgap (clautolisp 1.7.11): the
=cross-dialect-encoding-roundtrip-table= test now SKIPS the CP-1252 rows on
a host whose CL lacks the codec (=%host-supports-open-encoding-p=), so the
suite is green — but the *builtin itself* still errors for CCL users.

* Proposal

Give clautolisp its OWN CP-1252 codec so behaviour is identical on every
host. CP-1252 is ISO-8859-1 except the 0x80–0x9F block (27 defined code
points incl. € = 0x80 ↔ U+20AC, ‚ ƒ „ … † ‡ ˆ ‰ Š ‹ Œ Ž ' ' " " • – — ˜ ™ š
› œ ž Ÿ; 0x81 0x8D 0x8F 0x90 0x9D undefined). Options, prefer (1):

1. A small Gray-stream codec (character-input/-output) wrapping the octet
   stream: write = Unicode→CP-1252 byte via a 32-entry override table over
   Latin-1; read = byte→Unicode via the inverse. Route OPEN through it only
   when the resolved external-format is =:windows-1252= AND the host lacks
   the codec (keep the native path where it exists). Validate the whole
   autolisp-file surface (read-char/read-line/princ/close/file-position).
2. On CCL only, register the encoding via =ccl:define-character-encoding=
   with the 1252 decode table (host-specific; less portable than (1)).

* Acceptance criteria

- =(open f "w" "CP-1252")= writes € as byte 0x80 and é as 0xE9 on SBCL AND
  CCL; the reverse read round-trips both.
- =cross-dialect-encoding-roundtrip-table= runs (not skips) all CP-1252 /
  windows-1252 rows on every supported host; remove the skip stopgap.
- No regression in the file-encoding / open test suites on either host.

* Resolution — clautolisp 1.8.28

Implemented as option (1), a Gray-stream codec, with one deliberate
departure from the plan above: the 256-entry tables are BUILT FROM BABEL
rather than typed in by hand. babel was already a dependency of this
repository (alfe transcodes -Esource code pages with it) and already
ships CP-125x tables that are byte-identical on SBCL and CCL, so a
hand-written table here would only have created a second source of truth
free to drift from the first.

New file =clautolisp/autolisp-reader/source/external-format.lisp=:

- =OPEN-WITH-EXTERNAL-FORMAT= — CL:OPEN with the fallback spliced in.
  When the host ships the codec it is CL:OPEN verbatim, so the native
  path keeps its own performance, line-ending machinery and error
  signalling and nothing changes on SBCL.
- =EXTERNAL-FORMAT-AVAILABLE-P= — the predicate every "can we do this
  encoding?" site now asks. This mattered more than expected; see below.
- single-octet input/output Gray streams (read-char / unread-char /
  listen / clear-input / write-char / line-column / force-output /
  finish-output / close / element-type / open-stream-p), plus
  =UNENCODABLE-CHARACTER-ERROR=, a STREAM-ERROR like the host's own
  encoding error so callers that handle one handle both.
- =:NEWLINE= (:LF / :CR / :CRLF) is honoured, so the -e CP-1252-dos
  spelling works on the fallback path too.

Scope is limited to SINGLE-OCTET encodings (babel
ENC-MAX-UNITS-PER-CHAR = 1 and ENC-CODE-UNIT-SIZE = 8). That is not a
shortcut: measured, it covers 100% of the gap, and it is the range over
which byte offset and character offset coincide, so the wrapper needs no
decoder state. An encoding neither host nor babel ships (CP-950) still
fails as it does today rather than half-working.

** The measured gap

| encoding         | SBCL | CCL | babel |
|------------------+------+-----+-------|
| CP-1252          | yes  | NO  | yes   |
| CP-1250/51/53/54 | yes  | NO  | yes   |
| KOI8-R           | yes  | NO  | yes   |
| CP-932           | yes  | yes | yes   |
| CP-936           | yes  | yes | NO    |
| CP-950           | NO   | NO  | NO    |

So the fix is not CP-1252-shaped, it is CP-125x-and-KOI8-shaped; the
same mechanism closes all of them at once.

** Three call sites, not one

Fixing OPEN alone would have left the feature unreachable, because two
other places asked the host CL directly whether an encoding existed:

- =autolisp-cli= =%IMPLEMENTATION-KNOWS-ENCODING-P= — the -e validator.
  Before, =-e cp1251= was a CLI *usage error* on CCL while the same
  encoding named from AutoLISP code would now have worked. Typo
  protection is intact: =-e uft-8= is still rejected.
- =autolisp-builtins-core= =%HOST-CL-SUPPORTS-ENCODING-P= — would have
  fired ENC-UNSUPPORTED-TARGET on CCL for CP-1252 immediately before an
  OPEN that succeeds, i.e. warned the user off an operation that works.
- LOAD: =decode-and-normalize-file= had the identical hole, so
  =(load "f" "cp1252")= is fixed too.

** Bug found and fixed in passing

=--list-encodings= called =CCL::MAP-CHARACTER-ENCODINGS=, a function
that exists in no CCL release: the command died with an
undefined-function on every CCL host. It now walks
=CCL::*CHARACTER-ENCODINGS*= (133 entries), and the catalogue gained a
"Provided by clautolisp" section — 12 entries on CCL, absent on SBCL.

** Verification

- Acceptance criterion 1, at the byte level on BOTH hosts:
  € -> 0x80, é -> 0xE9, both round-tripping. € is the discriminating
  character; é alone would not prove the right codec ran, since CP-1252
  and ISO-8859-1 agree there.
- Acceptance criterion 2: the skip stopgap is gone;
  =cross-dialect-encoding-roundtrip-table= runs all 8 rows on CCL
  (3 were skipped before) and the whole tree reports 0 skips.
- Acceptance criterion 3: SBCL 6327 checks / CCL 6330 checks, 0
  failures; file-compat 84 scenarios / 166 checks on both; alfe 584
  checks on both.

The one alfe check that fails under CCL
(BRICSCAD-WINDOWS-BATCH-ARGV-...) was verified to fail identically on a
clean =origin/master= worktree — pre-existing and unrelated; filed as
=alfe-bricscad-argv-template-ccl.issue=.

** Follow-on

=run-file-compat/launch.lisp= did not load quicklisp, so the new
third-party dependency surfaced there as an ASDF MISSING-DEPENDENCY.
Fixed in the launcher. The Dockerfile and both CI setups already
preinstalled babel and trivial-gray-streams, so no CI dependency work
was needed.
